{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And the sign or the portent comes.</b> The vav in place of 'or,' that is, either the sign or the portent, for example, 'strikes his father and his mother,' and many more. It is taught: Rabbi Akiva said, far be it that HaShem would cause His presence to rest upon transgressors of His will; rather, it speaks of a prophet who at the outset was a true prophet and in the end became a false prophet, such as Hananiah son of Azzur. Thus Scripture speaks, and this is what the verse says: If a prophet arises in your midst — a true prophet — and while he is still a true prophet he gives you a sign or portent regarding things that HaShem told him — <b>and the sign or the portent comes</b> that he spoke of — and afterward he becomes a false prophet and says to you, 'Worship idolatry' — you might rely upon him because of the many signs you saw performed through him. Therefore it says, 'You shall not heed the words of that prophet.' And if you ask: since his end is to cause ruin, why did HaShem put His spirit upon him to be a true prophet, lest he lead His children astray? The answer to this: 'For HaShem your Elohim is testing you,' etc. How much more so if he performs the sign by means of sorcery, or by means of sleight of hand, or by means of deception — for example, he heard the words of a true prophet and went and said the same thing in the name of idolatry — you shall not heed him, but after HaShem your Elohim you shall walk."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:52:42.323488426Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}